# The scene that opts in to the FFT ocean — ADR 0076, and the only thing this
# slice moves.
#
# **`wave_model = "spectrum"` is the whole feature at this point.** Every other
# water scene in the repository omits the field, gets `gerstner`, and renders
# and simulates bit for bit as it did — the same shape of opt-in
# `ParticleEmitter.gpu` used, and the reason the wiring can land one readable
# commit at a time instead of moving fourteen golden references and every water
# scene's determinism hash at once.
#
# **The cascade layout is not authored here and that is deliberate.** A scene
# says which model; how many cascades, at what patch size and over which band
# of wavenumbers, is `loom_water`'s `shipping_stack`. Bands that must meet
# exactly are the kind of number an author gets wrong silently — a gap loses
# energy, an overlap double-counts it — so per-scene cascades wait for a slice
# of their own.
#
# # The sea this asks for
#
# **Twenty feet: `Hs = 6.10 m`.** The pair that realises it is `U10 = 18 m/s`
# with `fetch = 440 000 m`, and neither number is free —
# `docs/design/SEA-REBUILD.md` §3.6 has both derivations:
#
# - `U10 = 18` because the *fully-developed* ceiling at 16 m/s is
#   `0.22·U²/g = 5.74 m`, so no fetch at all reaches 6.10 from below 16.
# - `fetch = 440 000` and not the 376 000 an analytic detour once argued for.
#   Three standard parameterisations of the same sea disagree by 35%, and this
#   engine measured — `significant_height(wave_set_fetch(18.0, F))` — answers
#   5.638 m at 376 km and 6.099 m at 440 km. When the question is what this
#   engine does, run this engine.
#
# **Those 440 km are split 420 + 20 between a swell and the wind sea, and the
# split is exact rather than tuned.** A fetch-limited `Hs` is
# `0.0016·U·√(F/g)`, so `Hs ∝ √F` at a fixed wind and `m0 ∝ F` — fetches
# add. 5.959 m of swell and 1.300 m of wind sea in quadrature is 6.0994 m,
# which is `0.0016·18·√(440000/9.81)` to every digit either is quoted to.
# Nothing here was chosen to make the height come out; the height could not
# have moved.
#
# Both halves are genuinely fetch-limited — this wind is fully developed at
# 624 km — so both are *steeper* than the Pierson–Moskowitz limit rather than
# merely bigger, which is the property that makes a sea break instead of zoom.
#
# **What the split buys is a sea that breaks at all.** One fetch cannot carry
# both a twenty-foot height and a short steep tail. Measured over this scene's
# own sea at tick 400, 160,801 points across the 2048 m tile
# (`play::tests::the_crossing_sea_breaks_and_its_trace_over_reports_it`):
#
#                            realised Hs   mean mu_max   past 0.45
#     440 km in one sea      5.957 m       0.0515        1 point   0.0006%
#     420 + 20 at 60°        5.897 m       0.0842        162       0.1007%
#
# **One point of 160,801 becomes 162**, with 63% more mean compression behind
# it. The height is a property of the long waves and the breaking is a
# property of the short ones, and a single fetch has to choose.
#
# **The realised heights are single draws and 5.957 is not a contradiction of
# 6.0994.** This spectrum is narrow, so most of its energy lands in few cells
# and one seed is a small sample: `ocean::tests::
# the_realised_sea_is_the_size_the_spectrum_promised` measures the single-draw
# spread at 0.35x to 1.68x of the analytic value and averages 200 seeds to get
# 6.100 m. The scene has one seed. The 1% between the two rows above is that
# noise, not a change in energy — the energies are equal by construction.
#
# # The crossing, and why 60°
#
# The wind blows toward 15° and the swell runs toward 75° — `[cos 75°,
# sin 75°]`, which is `[0.25881904, 0.9659258]`, the wind's own pair swapped
# because 15° and 75° are complementary.
#
# **60° is a ceiling as much as a choice.** A grid cell holds one complex
# amplitude and therefore one direction of travel, and the directional spread
# is a magnitude, so a swell authored more than 90° off the wind is realised
# on the right *axis* travelling the wind's way along it. A genuinely opposed
# sea needs two grids or a per-cell direction and neither exists. 50–70° is a
# real crossing well inside what one grid can represent.
#
# And it is the case `WaterSample::mu_max` was written for and had never met:
# the largest eigenvalue of the horizontal compression rather than its trace
# (`WaterSample::fold`), because two seas compressing two axes at once *add
# their traces* while neither axis has folded. Measured here, past 0.45:
#
#                            eigenvalue   trace
#     420 + 20, swell along  0.1766%      0.9129%
#     420 + 20, swell at 60° 0.1007%      0.8800%
#
# **Turning the swell across the wind drops the eigenvalue 43% and the trace
# 3.6%.** The trace is very nearly blind to the difference between a sea
# running one way and a sea running two, and on the crossing sea 89% of the
# whitecaps it would paint are on water where neither axis has folded. That
# decision was made long before this engine had a crossing sea to make it on;
# this scene is the first one it has.
#
# **`Wind.speed` is not `U10`.** The authored field is the free-stream value
# the height profile saturates toward, and at the meteorological standard 10 m
# the field is 0.9083 of it — so `19.8165` measures `mean_speed_at(10.0) =
# 17.999987`. Anything that changes `ground_drag` here changes `U10` with it.
#
# **Deep water, no bed.** There is no `VoxelVolume` and `attenuation_depth`
# stays at its default zero, so nothing shoals and the surface is the open
# ocean everywhere in frame. A 6 m sea over a shallow bed is a different
# physical problem and not the one this scene is the acceptance test for.
#
# The camera sits at 7 m — a small ship's bridge over crests that reach about
# 5.6 m, so the near field is water rather than a horizon line. A distant orbit
# would frame the whole sea and photograph none of it; the spectral tail is
# exactly the detail that disappears first at range.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "5c2e9a41-7b38-4d6f-a1c0-8e3f2b95d074"

[[node]]
name = "Sea"

  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.26, 0.38, -0.89]
  sun_strength = 1.15
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.95, 0.86]
  ambient = 0.5
  sky_zenith = [0.13, 0.25, 0.47]
  sky_horizon = [0.55, 0.62, 0.70]
  fog_density = 0.004
  fog_falloff = 0.03

  # Beaufort 8. **`speed` is free-stream, not U10, and 19.8165 is a measured
  # number — do not tidy it to 20.** `loom_field::wind` warns three times that
  # the authored field is the value the height profile saturates toward, and at
  # the meteorological standard 10 m the profile is
  # `ground_drag + (1 - ground_drag)·10/12 = 0.90833` of it. Measured through
  # `Wind::mean_speed_at(10.0)`:
  #
  #     speed 19.80   -> U10 17.984999
  #     speed 19.8165 -> U10 17.999987   <- this
  #     speed 19.83   -> U10 18.012249
  #
  # A round 20.0 is `U10 = 18.167`, which is a different sea and a different
  # `Hs`. Changing `ground_drag` below changes U10 with it.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 15.0
  speed = 19.8165
  gustiness = 1.3
  turbulence = 1.0
  ground_drag = 0.45

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Sea"

  # No `waves` table: the spectrum builds the whole surface, and authoring a
  # wave list beside it is refused at load rather than silently dropped.
  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  wave_model = "spectrum"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1025.0
  drag = 2.0
  # The wind sea: short, steep, and 20 km of the 440 the scene's energy is.
  fetch = 20000.0

    # **Jerlov III — a scattering sea, and the reason this water is teal
    # rather than navy.** `ocean_fft.loom` is the measurement scene and keeps
    # the default, which is pure water: the clearest sea that physically
    # exists, whose backscatter is molecular and whose deep colour therefore
    # comes out green-to-blue 0.108 — navy, nearly black.
    #
    # The numbers are one row of the Jerlov table on `WaterOptics`, which
    # carries the sources and the derivation. **They are not a colour grade.**
    # `R∞ = 0.33·b_b/(K_d + b_b)` stays derived in the shader, and what these
    # two do is move it from (0.00034, 0.00421, 0.03915) to
    # (0.01150, 0.05609, 0.08991) — **green-to-blue 0.108 -> 0.624**, past the
    # 0.42 where navy becomes teal, with thirteen times the green.
    #
    # **What that is worth at THIS camera is small, and the honest number is
    # here rather than in a report nobody reads.** The lens is 3.4 m up and
    # 40 m back, so almost every water pixel is at a grazing angle where
    # Fresnel returns the sky and not the water. Measured over the bottom 80
    # rows of a 960x600 render at `--sim 400`:
    #
    #                    R     G     B     G/B
    #     pure water    46.0  53.0  68.9  0.769
    #     Jerlov III    49.1  65.5  79.7  0.822
    #
    # Twelve levels of green in the near field, three in the far. To see what
    # these coefficients actually do, look at `ocean_tropical.loom`, which is
    # framed for it.
    [node.components.WaterBody.optics]
    attenuation = [0.3828, 0.0911, 0.0719]
    backscatter = [0.01382, 0.01866, 0.02694]

    # The other 420 km, arrived from 60° to starboard as a swell — the
    # remnant of a wind that blew somewhere else and has since left, which is
    # why it carries its own `u10` and its own heading and takes nothing from
    # the scene's `Wind`.
    #
    # `u10` is the same 18 m/s because this is one storm's energy divided in
    # two, not two storms; a swell under a *different* wind is the other thing
    # this field can say and is not what this scene is the acceptance test for.
    [node.components.WaterBody.swell]
    u10 = 18.0
    fetch = 420000.0
    direction = [0.25881904, 0.9659258]

# One object with a known size, because a still of open water has no scale in
# it — a 6 m sea and a 0.6 m chop photograph identically without something to
# measure against. Two metres across, moored at 24 m.
#
# **And it floats, which is this scene's acceptance test rather than its
# decoration.** ADR 0076's whole argument is that the FFT surface stays on the
# force path — buoyancy reads the same water the GPU draws — and the way that
# fails is exactly the way `loom_water`'s module docs open: "a boat floating
# visibly above the water it is drawn on, with nothing to blame". A buoy
# hovering over a trough means the CPU and the GPU disagree about where the
# water is; a buoy sitting *in* the surface and rising with it means they
# agree. Until the render half lands the CPU side is what can be checked, and
# `loom water --at 3.5,-24 --sim N` beside `loom sim --assert` is how.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BOAT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The hull, its paint and its sixteen pontoons, from the file that owns them.
# Nothing about the boat is authored here — an instance plus a transform, the
# same way `fishing.loom` takes her.
[[prefab]]
key = "jib_vi"
id = "3c7a5e12-8b64-4f09-a2d3-6e1b90c47f58"
path = "../prefabs/jib_vi.loom"

# **This scene exists to be looked at, and that is the whole of its job.**
# `ocean_fft.loom` is the measurement scene — a buoy of known size on open
# water, because a still of the sea has no scale in it. This is the same sea
# with the thing the sea was built for sitting in it.
#
# A nineteen-metre hull is its own scale bar. At `Hs = 6.10 m` the sea is a
# third of her length, which is a number that means nothing written down and
# everything when you watch her take one over the bow.
#
# She floats on the same tiles the shader displaces from — that is ADR 0076's
# whole argument, and the way it fails is the way `loom_water`'s module docs
# open: "a boat floating visibly above the water it is drawn on, with nothing
# to blame". If she rides clear of her own water here, that is the bug, and it
# is visible without measuring anything.
[[node]]
name = "Boat"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0] }
prefab = "jib_vi"

# **Off her starboard quarter and low.** A sea reads as flat from above and as
# a wall from the waterline; three metres up and forty back is the height a
# person on another boat would see her from, which is the only view that shows
# both the swell she is riding and the chop crossing it.
#
# Yawed 18 degrees so she sits across the frame rather than end-on, and pitched
# down a little because the horizon is where the sea's size is legible.
[[node]]
name = "Camera"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [-34.0, 3.4, 26.0], rot_euler = [-4.0, -32.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.Camera]
  fov_y_degrees = 50.0
